#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065419 :tutorial/content "To solve a linear equation with variables on both sides, we bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n5x = 2x + 12\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that we have $5x$ on the left-hand side and $2x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $\\color{blue}2x$ from the right-hand side. We can do this by *subtracting* it from both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =2x+12 \\\\ 5x-2x & =2x+12-2x \\\\ 3x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=4$ is our answer, and we're done. \n\nWe can check that $x=4$ is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & \\overset{?}{=}2x+12 \\\\ 5(4) & \\overset{?}{=}2(4)+12 \\\\ 20 & =20\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2c184ea-e365-5696-9066-7ea9d9b0f03f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8129 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065420 :tutorial/content "Let's now solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n9x -9= 1+4x\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $9x$ on the left-hand side and $4x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $4x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}4x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-9 & =1+4x \\\\ 9x-9-4x & =1+4x-4x \\\\ 5x-9 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-9$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}9}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-9 & =1 \\\\ 5x-9+9 & =1+9 \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =10 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is our answer. We can check that this is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-9 & =1+4x \\\\ 9(2)-9 & \\overset{?}{=}1+4(2) \\\\ 18-9 & \\overset{?}{=}9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee887dc8-c657-52b2-a4e8-358afc2d1c4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8130 :tutorial/title "Solving Equations With Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}]}
